# Explore the Algebraic Genome of Nine Points

Begin with the [Steiner system](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steiner_system) hidden inside the nine-element group $(\mathbb Z/3\mathbb Z)^2$. Pick two points and let the first widget compute their unique zero-sum completion.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

{{demo:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the completion theorem</summary>
For $T(x,y)=-(x+y)$, commutativity gives $T(x,y)=T(y,x)$ and cancellation gives $T(x,T(x,y))=y$. If $3x=0$ for every point and $x\ne y$, then the completion differs from both inputs. Thus every pair determines exactly one three-point block.
</details>

## Move the whole geometry

Translation does not break a block: $T(x+t,y+t)=T(x,y)+t$. Explore all three translations along a sample direction, then inspect the complete finite check.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:0}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Why exponent three matters</summary>
Expanding gives $T(x+t,y+t)=-(x+y)-2t$. The identity $3t=0$ is equivalent to $-2t=t$, producing the translated completion.
</details>

## Maps that inherit the pattern

Every zero-preserving additive map commutes with $T$. The algorithm below turns this theorem into a finite diagnostic, while the next demonstration checks all $81$ linear endomorphisms of the nine-point group.

{{algorithm:2}}

{{demo:2}}

{{visualization:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the preservation argument</summary>
An additive map preserves negatives because $f(-a)+f(a)=f(0)=0$. Hence $f(-(x+y))=-(f(x)+f(y))$. This is the entire mechanism behind preservation by additive right multiplication.
</details>

## Preservation beside nonassociativity

A Hall-type multiplication can preserve every additive triple under right multiplication while retaining a proper left nucleus. Use the general cubic-time nucleus algorithm to locate elements that fail to associate universally.

{{algorithm:1}}

<details><summary>Definition of the left nucleus</summary>
The left nucleus is the set of all $a$ such that $a\circ(b\circ c)=(a\circ b)\circ c$ for every $b,c$. One associativity counterexample excludes its leftmost element and proves the nucleus proper.
</details>

## Watch the symmetry deficit grow

For Hall-family parameter $q\ge3$, the projective-linear benchmark exceeds the Hall collineation order, and the regularized ratio is at least $q^4$.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

{{algorithm:0}}

For broader context, see introductions to [finite geometry](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Finite_geometry), [non-Desarguesian planes](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Non-Desarguesian_plane), and [category theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category_theory).

